Feature: Automate migration of eligible files from Purgo S3 landing folder to archive folder using Databricks PySpark

  Background:
    Given Databricks workspace is configured with Unity Catalog "purgo_databricks" and schema "purgo_playground"
    And AWS credentials "access_key" and "secret_key" are stored in Databricks secret scope "aws_keys"
    And the table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains file migration metadata

  Scenario Outline: Migrate eligible files from S3 landing to archive folder
    Given the script reads all rows from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log"
    And the following file metadata is present:
      | file_name      | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date |
      | <file_name>    | <s3_landing_path>                        | <s3_archive_path>                        | <file_status> | <file_processed_date> |
    When the script filters for files where file_status = 'SUCCESS'
    And for each eligible file, it reads s3_landing_path and s3_archive_path
    And it uses AWS credentials from secret scope "aws_keys" to access S3
    And it moves the file "<file_name>" from "<s3_landing_path>" to "<s3_archive_path>"
    Then the file "<file_name>" is present in "<s3_archive_path>"
    And the file "<file_name>" is no longer present in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And no changes are made to "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" table

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                         | s3_archive_path                                         | file_status | file_processed_date      |
      | patient_001.csv   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw                 | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive             | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-01T10:00:00Z     |
      | lab_202406.csv    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/lab_raw                     | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/lab_archive                 | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-02T11:00:00Z     |
      | visit_123.json    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/visit_raw                   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/visit_archive               | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-03T12:00:00Z     |
      | patient_002.csv   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw                 | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive             | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-04T13:00:00Z     |
      | imaging_abc.dcm   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/imaging_raw                 | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/imaging_archive             | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-05T14:00:00Z     |

  Scenario Outline: Skip files with file_status not equal to 'SUCCESS'
    Given the script reads all rows from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log"
    And the following file metadata is present:
      | file_name      | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date |
      | <file_name>    | <s3_landing_path>                        | <s3_archive_path>                        | <file_status> | <file_processed_date> |
    When the script filters for files where file_status = 'SUCCESS'
    Then the file "<file_name>" is not migrated
    And the file remains in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And no changes are made to "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" table

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                                         | s3_archive_path                                         | file_status | file_processed_date      |
      | patient_003.csv   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw                 | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive             | FAILED      | 2024-06-06T15:00:00Z     |
      | lab_202407.csv    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/lab_raw                     | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/lab_archive                 | IN_PROGRESS | 2024-06-07T16:00:00Z     |
      | visit_124.json    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/visit_raw                   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/visit_archive               | ERROR       | 2024-06-08T17:00:00Z     |

  Scenario Outline: Error when required fields are missing in log table
    Given the script reads a row from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" with missing required fields
    And the following file metadata is present:
      | file_name      | s3_landing_path      | s3_archive_path      | file_status | file_processed_date |
      | <file_name>    | <s3_landing_path>    | <s3_archive_path>    | <file_status> | <file_processed_date> |
    When the script attempts to process the file
    Then an error is raised: "<error_message>"
    And the file is not migrated

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date      | error_message                                 |
      | patient_004.csv   | NULL                                      | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-09T18:00:00Z     | Missing s3_landing_path for file patient_004.csv |
      | lab_202408.csv    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/lab_raw       | NULL                                        | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-10T19:00:00Z     | Missing s3_archive_path for file lab_202408.csv  |
      | visit_125.json    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/visit_raw     | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/visit_archive   | NULL        | 2024-06-11T20:00:00Z     | Missing file_status for file visit_125.json      |

  Scenario Outline: Error when S3 path is invalid or inaccessible
    Given the script reads a row from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" with valid metadata
    And the following file metadata is present:
      | file_name      | s3_landing_path      | s3_archive_path      | file_status | file_processed_date |
      | <file_name>    | <s3_landing_path>    | <s3_archive_path>    | <file_status> | <file_processed_date> |
    When the script attempts to move the file and the S3 path is invalid or inaccessible
    Then an error is raised: "<error_message>"
    And the file is not migrated

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date      | error_message                                 |
      | patient_005.csv   | s3://invalid-bucket/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-12T21:00:00Z     | S3 path not found: s3://invalid-bucket/purgo/patient_raw |
      | lab_202409.csv    | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/lab_raw      | s3://invalid-bucket/purgo/lab_archive       | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-13T22:00:00Z     | S3 path not found: s3://invalid-bucket/purgo/lab_archive |

  Scenario Outline: Error when AWS credentials are missing or invalid
    Given the script attempts to retrieve "access_key" and "secret_key" from Databricks secret scope "aws_keys"
    When the secret is missing or invalid
    Then an error is raised: "<error_message>"
    And no files are migrated

    Examples:
      | error_message                                         |
      | Databricks secret 'aws_keys/access_key' not found     |
      | Databricks secret 'aws_keys/secret_key' not found     |
      | Invalid AWS credentials provided                      |

  Scenario Outline: Error when file type is unsupported (optional validation)
    Given the script reads a row from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" with unsupported file type
    And the following file metadata is present:
      | file_name      | s3_landing_path      | s3_archive_path      | file_status | file_processed_date |
      | <file_name>    | <s3_landing_path>    | <s3_archive_path>    | <file_status> | <file_processed_date> |
    When the script attempts to move the file
    Then an error is raised: "<error_message>"
    And the file is not migrated

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date      | error_message                                 |
      | patient_006.exe   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw  | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-14T23:00:00Z     | Unsupported file type: .exe                    |

  Scenario Outline: Duplicate file_name in log table with different paths
    Given the script reads multiple rows from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" with duplicate file_name but different paths
    And the following file metadata is present:
      | file_name      | s3_landing_path      | s3_archive_path      | file_status | file_processed_date |
      | <file_name>    | <s3_landing_path>    | <s3_archive_path>    | <file_status> | <file_processed_date> |
    When the script processes each row independently
    Then each file is migrated from its respective s3_landing_path to s3_archive_path

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date      |
      | patient_007.csv   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw  | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-15T08:00:00Z     |
      | patient_007.csv   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw2 | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive2 | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-15T09:00:00Z     |

  Scenario: No eligible files to migrate
    Given the script reads all rows from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log"
    And no rows have file_status = 'SUCCESS'
    When the script filters for eligible files
    Then no files are migrated
    And the script completes without error

  Scenario: File already exists in archive folder
    Given the script reads a row from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" with file_status = 'SUCCESS'
    And the file "<file_name>" already exists in "<s3_archive_path>"
    When the script attempts to move the file
    Then the file is not duplicated in "<s3_archive_path>"
    And the file is removed from "<s3_landing_path>"
    And no error is raised

    Examples:
      | file_name         | s3_landing_path                          | s3_archive_path                          | file_status | file_processed_date      |
      | patient_008.csv   | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_raw  | s3://bucket/filestore/purgo/patient_archive | SUCCESS     | 2024-06-16T10:00:00Z     |

  Scenario: File processed_date is not used for migration eligibility
    Given the script reads a row from "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" with file_status = 'SUCCESS' and any file_processed_date
    When the script processes the file
    Then the file is migrated regardless of file_processed_date

  Scenario: Migration does not update log table
    Given the script migrates files from s3_landing_path to s3_archive_path
    When the migration completes
    Then no updates are made to "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" table

# END
